# CONTEXT.md Management Rules
This repository uses scoped `CONTEXT.md` files in folders across the codebase to define localized boundaries, patterns, and expectations.
## Reading and Respecting Context
1. Before editing or adding files in any subdirectory, check for a `CONTEXT.md` file in that specific directory or its parent directories.
2. Specific rules defined in a sub-folder's `CONTEXT.md` override or narrow root-level guidelines for files within that scope.
3. Strictly follow the local dependency and export rules declared in the nearest `CONTEXT.md`, such as public exports and restricted imports between feature directories.
## Creating and Updating CONTEXT.md Files
1. When creating a directory intended to hold source code or components, automatically create a concise `CONTEXT.md` inside the directory using the folder context template below.
2. If introducing new core files, refactoring exported interfaces, or altering directory responsibilities, update the corresponding local `CONTEXT.md` to keep the context accurate.
3. A `CONTEXT.md` belongs in every source folder that holds a distinct responsibility, not only in the folder at the top of a package or module. When a folder has subfolders that each have their own `CONTEXT.md`, the parent file names each subfolder on one line and says what that subfolder is for, instead of naming the files inside the subfolders.
## Keeping Each CONTEXT.md Short
1. Each `CONTEXT.md` must stay under 5,500 bytes. When a `CONTEXT.md` goes above 5,500 bytes, shrink the `CONTEXT.md` down to 4,500 bytes. Do not cap a `CONTEXT.md` by a number of lines.
2. A rule is one sentence in the present tense saying what must hold now. History stays in the issue tracker, and a rule keeps only a bare link to that history. A `CONTEXT.md` is never a changelog.
3. When a fact already lives somewhere else, link to that place instead of copying the fact. One fact has one authoritative place.
## Required Sections
1. **Purpose:** One or two sentences saying what the folder is responsible for.
2. **Key Exports & Entry Points:** What another folder or package may import from the folder, and the one command that runs the folder if there is one. List individual files only when the folder has no public interface.
3. **Rules:** What must not be broken. Prefer rules that name a boundary, such as which folder must not import another folder or which shape must not be restated.
4. **Background:** The reasons and links behind the rules above, when a reason is too long to sit inside the rule itself.
## Verification
When a repository has enough `CONTEXT.md` files that reading them all by hand is no longer practical, add a test that checks every package or workspace has a `CONTEXT.md`, the folder path in each heading equals the containing folder, the required sections are present and ordered, and the byte cap holds. Do not check whether Markdown link targets or named paths exist on disk.
## Folder Context Template
```markdown
# Directory Context: `/<relative-path>`
## Purpose
[One to two sentences describing what this folder handles]
## Key Exports & Entry Points
- `index.ts`: Main public interface for this module.
- `[subfolder]/`: [What that subfolder is for — see its own CONTEXT.md]
- Command to run this folder: `[command]`
## Rules
- Nothing here imports from [restricted-module].
- [Any specific local coding convention, one sentence, present tense]
## Background
- [Rule above] comes from [link to the issue or the document that proved it].
```
# Glossary of Heidrun
- Created: 11 October 2026
- Last updated: 13 October 2026
- Status: Maintained glossary for agents and tools
- Related framing: [Project instructions](../../AGENTS.md)
This document gives one short definition for each word that Heidrun uses for a part of its window. Use these words in the code, in the documents, and in the conversations. One word means one thing, and one thing has one word.
## The three levels that come from Herdr
A workspace holds tabs. A tab holds panes.
```
Workspace
└── Tab
└── Pane
```
### Workspace
One project folder. In most cases, the folder is one repository. A workspace holds tabs.
### Tab
One entry of the tab bar. A tab belongs to one workspace. A tab holds one or more panes.
### Pane
One terminal inside a tab. The terminal runs a shell or a coding agent, for example Claude Code or Codex. A pane belongs to one tab.
## The parts of the window
### Tab bar
The horizontal row at the top of the viewport, inside the border of the viewport. It shows the tabs of the selected workspace, one entry for each tab.
### Viewport
The main area of the window, between the left sidebar and the right sidebar. It has a border, like each sidebar. It holds the tab bar, the panes of the selected tab that are not pinned, and the column of pinned panes.
### Pinned pane
A pane that the developer keeps visible. The pin button in the header of the pane pins the pane. A pinned pane stays visible, with the same width, when the developer changes tab or workspace.
### Column of pinned panes
The column at the right edge of the viewport. It holds the pinned panes, one above the other, in every tab.
### Left sidebar
The panel on the left of the viewport. It holds sidebar sections.
### Right sidebar
The panel on the right of the viewport. It holds sidebar sections.
### Sidebar section
One part of a sidebar, for example the Needs you, the Scripts, the Source control, or the Files sidebar section. A sidebar holds several sidebar sections, and shows one sidebar section at a time.
### Source control sidebar section
The sidebar section of the left sidebar that shows the Git changes of the selected workspace, as the source control view of VS Code does. From the top: a header with the title "Source Control" and its buttons, the commit message box, the commit button, the group "Staged Changes", and the group "Changes". It shows only the local Git repository, nothing of GitHub or GitLab. It replaces the Git sidebar section.
### Code hosting sidebar section
The sidebar section of the left sidebar that shows what lives on GitHub or on GitLab for the repository of the selected workspace: a list of the open issues, and a list of the open pull requests (GitHub) or merge requests (GitLab). A row has three lines and no button. A click on a row opens an information card. The words for its search, such as "saved search", are in [glossary_code_hosting.md](glossary_code_hosting.md).
### Files sidebar section
The sidebar section of the left sidebar that shows the whole folder tree of the Git repository of the selected workspace. A click on a folder folds or unfolds it. A click on a file opens the file in the large Git window. A right click on a file opens a context menu that copies the path of the file. It only reads files.
### Chat
One saved conversation between the developer and a coding agent, for example Claude Code or Codex. The coding agent saves each chat in a file on the disk of the developer. A chat belongs to a workspace when the coding agent ran in the folder of that workspace. A chat is not a pane: the pane is the terminal, and the chat is the saved conversation.
### Chats sidebar section
The sidebar section of the right sidebar that lists the chats of the selected workspace. At its top, a header that stays in place holds a line with the selector that chooses the coding agent, the Chats sort dropdown menu, and the two view buttons, then the dynamic topic bar. Below the header, the list shows the title of each chat of that coding agent, with a summary of three lines under the title. Under the list, the buttons "Show less" and "Show more" change the number of chats.
### Chats sort dropdown menu
The dropdown menu at the top of the Chats sidebar section, at the left of the two view buttons. Its button shows the name of the chosen sort key, with an icon that shows the chosen order. It has two parts: "Sort by" chooses what the chats are sorted by (created on, last updated, messages from you, issues, pull requests, or files written), and "Order" chooses "Ascending" (the smallest value first) or "Descending" (the largest value first), for the sort key that was chosen. It looks like the sort dropdown menu of the list of issues of GitHub.
### Dynamic topic
One short label of one or two words that Heidrun computes from the content of a chat, for example "sidebar" or "cache". Nobody writes it by hand. A chat has one to three dynamic topics. The Chats sidebar section shows them on the row of the chat.
### Raw dynamic topic
A dynamic topic exactly as it comes out of one chat. With the method "Keyword", Heidrun computes it from this chat and the other chats of the list, and it never asks a coding agent. With the method "Language model", a coding agent finds it from the title and the first messages of this chat. The same subject can get several raw dynamic topics, for example "sidebar" and "side bar".
### Clustered dynamic topic
The name of a cluster, which is a group of raw dynamic topics that have a close meaning. A clustered dynamic topic gives one label to one subject. The option "Enable clustered dynamic topics" of the Settings window makes the dynamic topic bar and the rows of the chats show clustered dynamic topics instead of raw dynamic topics.
### Translation table
The table, saved on the disk in the file `topic_clusters.json`, that gives the cluster of each raw dynamic topic. A coding agent, `codex` or `claude`, fills it, one call for all the new raw dynamic topics. A saved row never changes, except at a rebuild.
### Dynamic topic source information
The data that Heidrun reads in a chat to compute its dynamic topics. It has four parts: the title of the chat, the first messages that the developer typed, the folders of the files that the coding agent wrote, and the titles of the GitHub issues and of the pull requests that the chat mentioned. The messages of the coding agent are not part of it.
### Dynamic topic extraction
The choice of the method that computes the dynamic topics: "Keyword" (from the words of the dynamic topic source information, without a language model) or "Language model" (from the title and the first messages of each chat, with a coding agent, `codex` or `claude`, that is installed on this computer). The Settings window holds the choice, in its section "Dynamic topics", under the title "Extraction method".
### Dynamic topic bar
The row of small rounded buttons in the header of the Chats sidebar section, below the line of the selector of the coding agent, the Chats sort dropdown menu, and the two view buttons. The first button, "All", shows every chat. Each other button is one dynamic topic, the strongest first, and shows only the chats that have the topic. An arrow at its end, and an arrow at its start, move the bar by three topics.
### Sidebar icon bar
The narrow vertical bar of icons at the outer edge of each sidebar, like the bar of icons of Visual Studio Code. It shows one icon for each sidebar section of that sidebar. A click on an icon shows that sidebar section. When the sidebar is collapsed, the sidebar icon bar is the only part of the sidebar that stays visible.
### Status bar
The horizontal row at the bottom of the window. It holds status bar sections.
### Status bar section
One part of the status bar. It shows one piece of information, for example the usage of Claude, the usage of Codex, or the Git branch. A click on a status bar section opens its information card.
### Information card
A small card that opens on a click, never when the mouse pointer only moves over its part. It gives more detail about that part, and it can hold buttons. It opens above a status bar section, at the left of a part of the right sidebar, at the right of a part of the left sidebar, or below a circled question mark of a page of the Settings window. The part gets a background while the pointer is over it and while the information card is open. A second click on the part, the key Escape, a click outside, and the loss of the keyboard focus close the information card. The files line of a chat in the Chats sidebar section has the first information card that opens at the left of a part of a sidebar.
### Workspace status bar section
The first status bar section, at the left end of the status bar. It shows the status dot and the name of the selected workspace. Its information card gives the details of the selected workspace, and lists the workspaces to select, rename, close, move, or create.
## Rules
- The word "tab" means only a tab of Herdr, as defined above. An icon of a sidebar icon bar is not a tab.
- The word "pane" means only a terminal inside a tab. A list of tabs is not a list of panes.
- The word "chat" means only a saved conversation, as defined above. A pane is not a chat.
- Write "dynamic topic bar" in full. Do not write "filter bar" or "chips" for it.
- Write "dynamic topic source information" in full.
- Write "raw dynamic topic" and "clustered dynamic topic" in full, and "translation table" for the table of the clusters. Do not write "category", "group name", or "mapping" for them. Do not write "source", "metadata", or "meta information" for it.
- The word "section" is never alone. Write "sidebar section" or "status bar section".
- Write "pinned pane" and "column of pinned panes". Do not write "docked pane", "side view", or "alongside".
# User interface of Heidrun: files
- Created: 13 October 2026
- Last updated: 13 October 2026
- Status: Maintained rules for agents and tools
- Related framing: [Project instructions](../../../../AGENTS.md)
- Related rules: [Glossary of Heidrun](../../glossary.md)
- Related issue: [issue 349](https://github.com/heidrun-org/heidrun/issues/349), [issue 383](https://github.com/heidrun-org/heidrun/issues/383)
This file is a part of the rules of the user interface of Heidrun. The words are defined in [glossary.md](../../glossary.md).
## Files
The Files sidebar section shows the whole folder tree of the Git repository of the selected workspace ([issue #349](https://github.com/heidrun-org/heidrun/issues/349)).
- The Files sidebar section changes nothing but one thing: it creates a new empty file or a new empty folder, because the developer asked for it ([issue #349](https://github.com/heidrun-org/heidrun/issues/349)). It has no button to rename, move, delete, or edit a file.
- The header stays at the top of the sidebar section while the rows scroll under it. It holds the title "Files" and five buttons that show only an icon, in this order: "New file", "New folder", "Refresh", "Collapse all folders" (the icon `dash-square`), and "More options" (three vertical dots).
- The menu of "More options" has three groups, and a check mark shows the choice that is on. "Indentation": Small (12 pixels for each level), Medium (20 pixels, the default), Large (28 pixels). "Row height": Compact (24 pixels), Comfortable (30 pixels, the default). "Indent guides": Show (the default), Hide. The choices are saved in the settings `filesPanelIndent`, `filesPanelRowHeight`, and `filesPanelIndentGuides`.
- The folder tree uses the same list of files as the Files window: the files that Git tracks, the new files, and the folders that `.gitignore` ignores only when the option to show ignored files is on, as folders that do not unfold. Folders come first, then files, in alphabetical order. Each row shows, from the left: one indent guide for each level of depth, a slot for the arrow of a folder, the icon of a folder or of the kind of the file, the name, and for a changed file a colored status letter at the right. A file keeps the slot of the arrow empty, so the names of the files and of the folders of one level line up. The icons are grey, not colored.
- "New file" and "New folder" show a name box inside the tree, as the first child of the target folder, indented as the children of that folder. The target folder is the selected folder, or the folder of the selected file, or the root of the repository when nothing is selected. The developer types only the name, and the name cannot contain the character `/`. The key Enter creates the file or the folder, and the key Escape, or a click outside the name box, cancels. A new file opens in the large Git window. A name that exists already gives an error message, and the name box stays open. The new file or the new folder becomes the selected row.
- Git does not list an empty folder, so the tree remembers the new empty folders that the developer created, until Heidrun restarts. At each reading of the tree, including the button "Refresh", the program asks the backend which of these folders still exist on the disk, and forgets the others, for example a folder that the developer removed in the Finder or in a terminal.
- "Collapse all folders" folds every folder. There is no button that unfolds every folder.
- A click on a file or on a folder selects its row. Only one row is selected. The selected row has a background that is darker than the background of a row under the pointer, and a blue line around it, as the selected row of VS Code. The selection belongs to the workspace: each workspace keeps its own selected row, and when the developer comes back to a workspace, its selected row is the one that the developer selected there, with the folders above it unfolded. The selection stays when the developer shows another sidebar section and comes back, and when the large Git window closes. A row under the pointer has a background that is lighter than the sidebar, in the dark theme and in the light theme: it has the color `--files-row-hover` (`#22262a` in the dark theme, `#ececE8` in the light theme), and the difference to the sidebar is at least 10 levels in one color channel, so the developer sees it without effort. A click on the empty space of the sidebar section, outside every row, selects nothing in the selected workspace only.
- A click on a folder folds or unfolds it. The folds stay when the developer changes the sidebar section.
- A right click on the row of a file opens a context menu at the pointer ([issue #383](https://github.com/heidrun-org/heidrun/issues/383)). A context menu is a small menu that opens at the pointer. It has two items, in this order: "Copy absolute path" puts the absolute path of the file on the clipboard, and "Copy relative path" puts the path of the file on the clipboard, relative to the folder of the workspace. After a copy, a toast shows the text that was copied. The key Escape, a click outside the context menu, or a click on an item closes the context menu. The context menu stays inside the window. The row of a folder has no context menu. A copy changes no file, so the Files sidebar section still only reads files.
- A click on a file opens the file in the large Git window, the same window as the Source control sidebar section. The large Git window shows a file that has no change as a whole file, with no diff, and disables the buttons "Diff" and "Side by side" for it.
- The tree is read again when the repository changes, when the number of changed files changes, and at a click on "Refresh".
- The icon `folder2` of the Files sidebar section is the first icon of the sidebar icon bar of the left sidebar, above the Scripts icon. It shows no number.
# User interface of Heidrun: workspace
- Created: 13 October 2026
- Last updated: 13 October 2026
- Status: Maintained rules for agents and tools
- Related framing: [Project instructions](../../../../AGENTS.md)
- Related rules: [Glossary of Heidrun](../../glossary.md)
This file is a part of the rules of the user interface of Heidrun. The words are defined in [glossary.md](../../glossary.md).
## Workspace
- One workspace is selected at a time.
- The workspace status bar section is the first status bar section, at the left end of the status bar. It shows a status dot and the name of the selected workspace. A click on it opens its information card above the section. The information card does not open when the mouse moves over the section.
- The status dot is always visible, and it sits before the name, in the same place as the dot of a tab. When an agent in another workspace is blocked or has finished, the dot has the color of that status, and it follows the cards of the Needs you sidebar section. Otherwise, the dot shows the status of the selected workspace. A workspace without an agent gets a hollow ring.
- The information card of the workspace status bar section gives the folder, the Git branch, the number of tabs, and the number of agents of the selected workspace. The folder is shown on one line, under the rules of the section "Text that must stay on one line" (in [layout.md](../window/layout.md)): while the pointer is over the information card, a folder that does not fit scrolls sideways to its end, and it shows its beginning again when the pointer leaves. Under the details, it lists the workspaces. A click on a workspace selects it, and closes the information card.
- The list of workspaces has no separator line between the workspaces, and no space between two rows: between the two separator lines, the pointer is always on a workspace. Each row is 32 pixels tall, with the padding inside the row. One separator line sits between the list and the entry "New workspace…" at the end of the information card.
- The line after the workspace status bar section has 6 more pixels of space on each side than the other lines of the status bar, so that the workspace status bar section is not packed against the next status bar section.
- Each workspace of the list offers the rename of the workspace, the close of the workspace, and the move of the workspace by drag and drop. The rename puts a text field in the place of the name in the status bar. A double click on the workspace status bar section does not rename the workspace.
- Two other ways to switch workspace: the command search with ⌘K, and the shortcut Option+Command+Up arrow or Option+Command+Down arrow.
- Heidrun saves the identifier of the selected workspace each time that the selection changes, in the option `selectedWorkspaceId` of the store `settings.ts`. At the next start, Heidrun selects this workspace again, with its last tab and its last pane. When this workspace does not exist any more, Heidrun selects the workspace that Herdr has focused, or the first workspace.
import { DocumentationPaths } from './documentation_paths.ts';
import type { DocumentationHintSet } from './documentation_types.ts';
///////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////
///////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////
//	DocumentationPrompt — writes the two prompts that tell the coding agent how to update the documentation
///////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////
///////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////
/**
* Builds the two prompts for the coding agent. The outline prompt asks for a plan of the pages, with no edit. The
* writing prompt asks the coding agent to write the pages from that plan. Both prompts hold the same sources, page
* types, style, and hints. The coding agent reads the documentation pages, the rule files, and the code itself,
* because it runs in the repository.
*/
export class DocumentationPrompt {
/** The four page types of the Diátaxis framework, as they are written in the comment `page type`. */
static readonly pageTypes = ['tutorial', 'how-to', 'reference', 'explanation'];
/**
* Builds the outline prompt: the first pass, which plans the pages and edits no file.
* @param hints The hints between the last documented commit and the current commit.
* @param onlyRuleFiles The rule files that limit the run, or an empty list for a full check.
* @returns The prompt, in Markdown.
*/
static buildOutline(hints: DocumentationHintSet, onlyRuleFiles: string[] = []): string {
const promptText = /* markdown */ `
You plan the user documentation of the application Heidrun. You run inside the Git repository of Heidrun. In this pass you DO NOT edit any file: you only read, and you answer with an outline. A second pass writes the pages from your outline.
${DocumentationPrompt._buildContextText(onlyRuleFiles)}
${DocumentationPrompt._buildPageTypesText()}
${DocumentationPrompt._buildStyleText()}
## Your final answer: the outline
Read the current pages, the rule files, and the glossary first. Then answer with the outline of ALL the pages that must exist after this run, and nothing else, in this form:
\`\`\`
### <file name, for example copy_a_file_path.md>
- Action: add | change | keep | remove
- Page type: tutorial | how-to | reference | explanation
- Title: <the title of level one>
- Reader goal: <one sentence: what the reader wants to do or know>
- Sources: <the rule files, relative to the root of the repository>
- Sections:
- <heading of level two>: <one sentence on what it says>
- Troubleshooting: <the symptoms that the sources show, or none>
- Related pages: <the file names of 2 to 4 other pages>
- Leave out: <the details of the rules that the reader does not need>
\`\`\`
Then add one last part "### Sidebar" with the groups and the pages of the sidebar, in order.
Check your outline before you answer: each page has one type and one reader goal, a how-to page holds one task or a few close tasks, and no page restates a rule file.
## Hints
${DocumentationPrompt._buildHintsText(hints)}
`;
return promptText.trim();
}
/**
* Builds the writing prompt: the second pass, which writes the pages from the outline of the first pass.
* @param hints The hints between the last documented commit and the current commit.
* @param onlyRuleFiles The rule files that limit the run, or an empty list for a full check.
* @param outline The outline that the first pass returned.
* @returns The prompt, in Markdown.
*/
static buildWriting(hints: DocumentationHintSet, onlyRuleFiles: string[], outline: string): string {
const { documentationFolder, sidebarConfigFile, generatedMark, rulesFolder } = DocumentationPaths;
const promptText = /* markdown */ `
You write the user documentation of the application Heidrun. You run inside the Git repository of Heidrun, and you may edit files. No person writes or edits a documentation page by hand: you own the generated pages.
## Task
A first pass planned the pages. Its outline is below. Write the pages of the outline: add, change, or remove each page as its action says, and update the sidebar. Follow the outline. Leave it only when a source shows that the outline is wrong, and say so in your report.
${DocumentationPrompt._buildContextText(onlyRuleFiles)}
${DocumentationPrompt._buildPageTypesText()}
${DocumentationPrompt._buildStyleText()}
## Rules for the files
- The first line after the title of every page that you write is exactly this comment: \`${generatedMark}\`. The next line is the page type, for example \`<!-- page type: how-to -->\`. Without these two comments, the test of the repository fails.
- Under every heading of level two, except "Related pages", write a comment that names the source of the section, for example \`<!-- source: ${rulesFolder}/sidebars/files.md -->\`. The path is relative to the root of the repository, and the file must exist. When a section has several sources, write one comment for each. When a fact comes only from the code and from no rule file, also name the code file in a source comment, so a reader of the page can check the fact.
- Do not edit the page \`${DocumentationPaths.shortcutsPageName}\`. The command \`pnpm docs:shortcuts\` generates it.
- Do not edit the file \`.last_documented_commit\`. The command writes it after you finish.
- Write each page with one title of level one. Link only to pages that exist after this run.
- In the sidebar in \`${sidebarConfigFile}\`, make one group for each page type that has pages, in this order: "Get started" (tutorial), "How-to guides", "Reference", "Explanation". The page \`${DocumentationPaths.shortcutsPageName}\` goes in "Reference". Remove the link of each removed page.
- Do not write raw angle brackets outside code, because VitePress reads \`<name>\` as a Vue tag and the build fails. Do not use tab characters in Markdown pages. Follow the repository TypeScript style in the sidebar configuration.
- Write each paragraph as one long line. Do not wrap lines.
- Reuse real screenshots supplied in \`packages/auxiliary/website_public/docs/public/documentation_images\` when they help the reader find a control or understand a step. Read the nearby screenshot metadata and match each image to the page and state it shows. Use Markdown images with descriptive alternative text and a URL starting with \`/documentation_images/\`. Keep screenshots close to the relevant steps or options. Never invent image paths or present generated artwork as the application. When no verified screenshot is supplied, do not add an image placeholder.
- Edit only files in \`${documentationFolder}\` and the file \`${sidebarConfigFile}\`.
## The outline to follow
${outline.trim()}
## Your final answer
Your final answer is a report of the generated file changes, in Markdown. It has three lists with the titles "Added", "Changed", and "Removed". Each item names a page or a section and gives the reason in one sentence. Write "none" under a title that has no item. Write nothing else.
## Hints
${DocumentationPrompt._buildHintsText(hints)}
`;
return promptText.trim();
}
///////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////
///////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////
//	Helpers
///////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////
///////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////
/**
* Writes the part of both prompts that says what to check, where to read, and what to trust.
* @param onlyRuleFiles The rule files that limit the run, or an empty list for a full check.
* @returns The text, in Markdown.
*/
private static _buildContextText(onlyRuleFiles: string[]): string {
const { documentationFolder, rulesFolder, glossaryFile, sidebarConfigFile } = DocumentationPaths;
const contextText = /* markdown */ `
## Scope
Git history is read only in this process. Never stage files, create or switch branches, write commits, push, merge, or create a pull request or merge request. The developer handles those actions outside this process. Leave generated files uncommitted. Do not run docs:update_by_ai or another documentation writer recursively. Use no subagents. Preserve existing local changes outside the files allowed by this task.
Do a full check of the user documentation. Compare ALL the documentation pages with ALL the current rules and with the code. For each feature of the application, add a page or a section for a new feature, change the section of a changed feature, remove the section of a removed feature, and do nothing for an internal change (refactor, test, build, developer tool). Remove a whole page when all its sections are removed.
The hints at the end only say which parts to look at first. Do not trust the hints as the truth.
${DocumentationPrompt._buildLimitText(onlyRuleFiles)}## Where to read
- The documentation pages are in the folder \`${documentationFolder}\`.
- The rules of the user interface are in the folder \`${rulesFolder}\`, in many small files.
- The glossary is the file \`${glossaryFile}\`. Use the words of the glossary, and no other word, for each part of the application.
- The code of the application is in the folder \`packages/apps\`. Read it when a rule is not enough.
- The sidebar of the website is in the file \`${sidebarConfigFile}\`.
## Source of truth, in this order
1. The rule files in \`${rulesFolder}\` and the glossary. The rules say what the application does now, so a removed feature is also removed from the rules.
2. The code of the application.
3. The pull requests and the issues in the hints. They say what changed and why. They are hints, not the source of truth. You may run \`gh issue view <number>\` to read an issue.
Write only what the sources show. Never invent a feature. Never write about a feature that no rule or code shows.
`;
return contextText.trim();
}
/**
* Writes the part of both prompts that explains the four page types of the Diátaxis framework.
* @returns The text, in Markdown.
*/
private static _buildPageTypesText(): string {
const pageTypesText = /* markdown */ `
## Page types
Each page has exactly ONE type, from the Diátaxis framework (https://diataxis.fr). Never mix two types in one page: a page that mixes steps and descriptions is the main reason why a page is hard to read.
- **tutorial**: a lesson for a new user. A short path of steps that leads to a first success with Heidrun. Only the page \`index.md\` is a tutorial.
- **how-to**: the steps that solve one task of a person who already uses Heidrun, for example "Copy the path of a file". The title starts with a verb. Most pages are how-to pages.
- **reference**: a dry description to look up, mostly tables: the buttons of a header, the items of a menu, the options of a window. No steps and no story.
- **explanation**: why a feature works the way it does, for example the state "Needs you". Write one only when a person cannot use a feature well without the idea.
### Troubleshooting
A how-to page ends with a section \`## Troubleshooting\` when the sources show a failure that the reader can meet: an error message, a button that is disabled, something that does not appear, or a change that is lost. Never invent a failure, and leave the section out when the sources show none. Write each problem in this shape:
- A heading of level three that says the symptom as the reader sees it, for example "The name box stays open".
- **Cause**: one sentence.
- **Solution**: numbered steps.
- **Check**: one sentence that says how the reader sees that it works now.
### Related pages
Every page ends with a section \`## Related pages\`: a bullet list of 2 to 4 links to the other pages that a reader of this page may need next, each with a few words that say why, for example \`- [Copy a file path](/documentation/copy_a_file_path): to paste the path in a terminal\`. A link starts with \`/documentation/\` and has no \`.md\` at the end. Link only to pages that exist after this run.
Organize the pages by what the reader wants to do, not by the rule files. A page can use several rule files, and a rule file can feed several pages: for example, the rule file of a sidebar section can give two how-to pages and one reference page.
`;
return pageTypesText.trim();
}
/**
* Writes the part of both prompts that says how a page must read.
* @returns The text, in Markdown.
*/
private static _buildStyleText(): string {
const styleText = /* markdown */ `
## Style: the pages must be easy to read
- Write for a person using Heidrun. Explain what the person can do and how, without describing how the program works internally.
- Start each page with one sentence that says what the reader can do or find there.
- Keep each page focused on its purpose. Avoid repeating information from another page; link to that page where it helps the reader continue.
- Write the steps of a task as a numbered list, with one action in each step. Include enough information to find the control and begin, then say what result to expect or how to check that the task worked.
- Name controls as they appear on screen. When a name alone is not enough to find a control, describe where it is or what it looks like. Put control names in bold.
- Explain an unfamiliar term in plain words the first time it appears. Use the same name for the same part of Heidrun throughout the page.
- Use a table when readers need to compare or look up several controls, choices, or meanings. Give each entry a clear name and a useful explanation.
- A paragraph has at most three sentences.
- Keep pages concise. Include details when they help a reader complete a task, choose an option, understand a result, or recover from a known problem. Do not add defaults, exact dimensions, internal implementation details, or remedies that are not supported by the sources.
- Make links useful for the next step: say what the linked page helps the reader do or find.
- Describe a known problem with its symptom and supported cause and solution. Include a check when the reader can verify that the solution worked.
`;
return styleText.trim();
}
/**
* Writes the part of the prompt that limits the run to some rule files.
* @param onlyRuleFiles The rule files that limit the run, or an empty list for a full check.
* @returns The text, with a blank line at the end, or an empty text for a full check.
*/
private static _buildLimitText(onlyRuleFiles: string[]): string {
if (onlyRuleFiles.length === 0) {
return '';
}
const fileLines = onlyRuleFiles.map((ruleFile) => {
return `- \`${ruleFile}\``;
});
return [
'## Limit of this run',
'',
'This run is NOT a full check. Plan and write only the pages for the features that these rule files describe, and the pages that name them as source. Do not add, change, or remove any other page.',
'',
...fileLines,
'',
'',
].join('\n');
}
/**
* Writes the part of the prompt that holds the hints.
* @param hints The hints between the last documented commit and the current commit.
* @returns The text of the hints, in Markdown.
*/
private static _buildHintsText(hints: DocumentationHintSet): string {
if (hints.lastDocumentedCommit === null) {
return 'This is the first run. There is no last documented commit, so there are no hints. Do the full check only.';
}
const pullRequestLines = hints.mergedPullRequests.map((pullRequest) => {
return `- #${pullRequest.number}: ${pullRequest.title}`;
});
const issueLines = hints.fixedIssueNumbers.map((issueNumber) => {
return `- #${issueNumber}`;
});
const fileLines = hints.changedFiles.map((changedFile) => {
return `- \`${changedFile}\``;
});
const truncatedLine = hints.isRulesDiffTruncated ? '\nThe diff is cut here because it is too long. Run `git diff` to read the rest.' : '';
const sections = [
`The last documented commit is \`${hints.lastDocumentedCommit}\`. The current commit is \`${hints.currentCommit}\`.`,
'### Merged pull requests since the last documented commit',
pullRequestLines.length === 0 ? 'none' : pullRequestLines.join('\n'),
'### Issues that these commits fix',
issueLines.length === 0 ? 'none' : issueLines.join('\n'),
'### Files changed in the application, the rules, and the glossary',
fileLines.length === 0 ? 'none' : fileLines.join('\n'),
'### Diff of the rules and of the glossary',
hints.rulesDiff === '' ? 'none' : `\`\`\`diff\n${hints.rulesDiff}\n\`\`\`${truncatedLine}`,
];
return sections.join('\n\n');
}
}
